Here is a simple example of how you can create a Rust application that supports the ElevenLabs.io API and reads the contents of the clipboard aloud using TTS (Text-to-Speech). This example uses the `reqwest` crate for making HTTP requests to the ElevenLabs.io API and the `tts` crate for text-to-speech functionality.

First, add the following dependencies to your `Cargo.toml` file:

```toml
[dependencies]
reqwest = "0.11"
tts = "0.1.0"
```

Then, create a new Rust file (e.g., `main.rs`) and add the following code:

```rust
use reqwest;
use tts::{Tts, Voice};

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    // Set your ElevenLabs.io API key
    let api_key = "YOUR_API_KEY_HERE";

    // Set the text to read aloud
    let text = std::env::var("clipboard").unwrap_or_else(|_| "Hello, World!".to_string());

    // Create a new TTS instance
    let tts = Tts::new(Voice::EnglishUs);

    // Make a request to the ElevenLabs.io API to get the audio URL
    let res = reqwest::get(format!("https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/synthesize?text={}&key={}", text, api_key))
        .await
        .unwrap();

    // Get the audio URL from the response
    let audio_url = res.json::<serde_json::Value>().await.unwrap()["audio_url"].as_str().unwrap();

    // Download the audio file
    let audio = reqwest::get(audio_url).await.unwrap();

    // Play the audio file using the TTS instance
    tts.play(audio).await.unwrap();
}
```

This code assumes that you have the `clipboard` environment variable set to the text you want to read aloud. If you don't have this variable set, it will default to "Hello, World!".

Please note that you need to replace `YOUR_API_KEY_HERE` with your actual ElevenLabs.io API key.

Also, this code uses the `tokio` crate for asynchronous programming, so you need to add it to your `Cargo.toml` file as well:

```toml
[dependencies]
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["full"] }
```

This is a basic example and you may need to modify it to fit your specific requirements. You can also use other TTS libraries or APIs if you prefer.

